          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of René Descartes</strong><br>\nRené Descartes was born in 1596 in La Haye en Touraine, France. From a young age, he was enrolled in the Jesuit College Royal Henry-Le-Grand at La Flèche. Here, he was introduced to mathematics and physics, including Galileo's work. Despite his health issues, Descartes was a bright student, and his education laid the foundation for his later philosophical and mathematical theories.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Service and Visions</strong><br>\nAfter his education, Descartes joined the army of Prince Maurice of Nassau. In 1619, while stationed in Germany, Descartes experienced a series of intense dreams or visions that he interpreted as a divine sign. These visions convinced him to dedicate his life to the pursuit of knowledge. This experience was pivotal in shaping his future contributions to philosophy and science.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Development of Analytical Geometry</strong><br>\nDescartes is often hailed as the father of analytical geometry. He discovered the possibility of describing geometric shapes through algebraic equations, which was a revolutionary idea at the time. This led to the Cartesian coordinate system, named after him, which is still used today to plot values in algebra and geometry.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
