          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Philosophical Contributions</strong><br>\nIn philosophy, Descartes is known for his skepticism and methodological doubt. His famous statement \"Cogito, ergo sum\" (\"I think, therefore I am\") reflects his belief that the act of thinking is proof of one's existence. This foundational idea became a cornerstone of Western philosophy and is still widely studied and debated.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Scientific Method</strong><br>\nDescartes also made significant contributions to the development of the scientific method. He advocated for a deductive approach to science, where reasoning from clear and distinct ideas could lead to scientific truths. His work provided a structured way to question and explore the natural world scientifically.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Publications and Ban</strong><br>\nSome of Descartes' most important works include \"Discourse on the Method\" and \"Meditations on First Philosophy.\" His ideas, however, were controversial. The Catholic Church banned his books soon after his death because they challenged traditional religious views about the soul and God's existence.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
