          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Years and Death</strong><br>\nDescartes spent his later years in Stockholm at the invitation of Queen Christina of Sweden. He found the Swedish climate harsh and the Queen's demands for early morning study taxing. Unfortunately, the combination of cold weather and overwork may have contributed to his death from pneumonia in 1650.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Mathematics</strong><br>\nBesides analytical geometry, Descartes' rule of signs is an important part of his legacy in mathematics. It provides a way to determine the number of positive and negative roots in a polynomial equation. His work in math was groundbreaking and influenced subsequent generations of mathematicians.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Lasting Legacy</strong><br>\nDescartes' influence extended beyond his lifetime, affecting many areas of modern philosophy and science. His thoughts on dualism, the separation of mind and body, and his belief in the primacy of reason over experience have shaped intellectual debates for centuries. His legacy is that of a thinker who sought to understand the natural world through a new lens of clarity and rationality.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
